=== tool delegate
Hands a task to a sub-thread of your own, which works on it with the same tools in this folder. Call it only when the person asks you to delegate work, to use sub-agents, or to work on several things at once — never on your own initiative. A new sub-thread sees nothing of this conversation: give `task` all it needs. You wait for it to finish and are given what it said last, several calls in one reply running at once; or, with `background`, you are answered at once and told when it finishes, for work that does not hold up yours. Every answer names the sub-thread, which `thread` gives more to do. What it says it did is not proof: look at what it changed before you report it done. The person sees each sub-thread, and can follow and steer it.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "background": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Run it in the background and be answered at once: you are told when the sub-thread finishes, by a message. Do\nnot wait for it, poll it or ask after its progress.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "task": {
      "description": "What the sub-thread is to do: the whole of it, as a new one sees nothing else.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "thread": {
      "description": "The id of a sub-thread of yours, running or finished, to give this task to: it keeps what it did. Omit to start\na new one.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "title": {
      "description": "A few words the person knows a new sub-thread by.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "task"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool edit
Replaces text in a file: `old` must match one place, or every place with `all`. Give in `old` just the lines that make it unique. It is matched exactly, else with typographic characters (curly quotes, dashes, special spaces) read as plain ones, else line by line ignoring trailing whitespace; a match not exact is noted with how it matched. Gives the change as a unified diff. A refusal gives its reason: empty, same (`new` is `old`), not-found (`old` is nowhere in the file; the lines nearest to it follow, as the file holds them, or a note that `new` is there already), unchanged, many (how many places matched: give more of `old`, or set `all`).
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "all": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Replace every match.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "new": {
      "description": "The text to put in its place.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "old": {
      "description": "The text to replace, as read gives it.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "path": {
      "description": "The file, relative to the workspace, or absolute; ~ the home folder.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path",
    "old",
    "new"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool execute
Runs JavaScript as the body of an async function, calling tools through `tools`: `await tools.read({ path: "a.txt" })` resolves to what the tool gives, as text, and rejects where the tool refuses. Call them in loops, at once with Promise.all, and work on what they give in code. Gives what the script returned, as JSON, and what it wrote with console.log. Use it for work of many steps: many files read or changed, a page's many actions, a batch extracted. `tools` holds browser, edit, glob, grep, read, shell, terminal, write, each taking the arguments its function does, and the MCP servers' tools of <catalog>.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "code": {
      "description": "JavaScript, run as the body of an async function: what it returns is what the script gives.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "code"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool glob
Finds the files under a directory whose paths match a glob, honouring ignore files: their paths one a line, the newest first, 200 a page; a note gives the offset to go on from.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "offset": {
      "description": "The first file, from 1, the newest first.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "default": "",
      "description": "The directory to look under, relative to the workspace, or absolute; ~ the home folder; the workspace unless\ngiven.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "pattern": {
      "description": "A glob, as src/**/*.rs.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "pattern"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool grep
Finds the lines a regular expression matches, in ripgrep's syntax, in the files under a directory or in one file, honouring ignore files: each as `path:line: text`, a line around it as `path:line- text`, 250 matches a page, a note giving the offset to go on from. A line longer than 500 characters is cut. Binary files are passed over, and counted.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "case_insensitive": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Match letters of either case.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "context": {
      "default": 0,
      "description": "Lines shown around each match.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 0,
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "glob": {
      "description": "Only files whose paths match it: their names, for a glob of no /.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "literal": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Take `pattern` as plain text.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "offset": {
      "description": "The first matching line, from 1, the files in order by path.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "default": "",
      "description": "The directory or file to look in, relative to the workspace, or absolute; ~ the home folder; the workspace\nunless given.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "pattern": {
      "description": "A regular expression, as ripgrep reads one, or plain text with literal.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "pattern"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool question
Asks the person some questions at once, each with options to choose one of, or several with `multiple`, or none to answer in words. They may answer in words of their own instead, leave any unanswered, or dismiss them all. Put the option you recommend first, "(recommended)" after its label. Ask only what you cannot find out yourself and what changes what you do.
--- parameters
{
  "$defs": {
    "Asked": {
      "description": "A question.",
      "properties": {
        "header": {
          "description": "What it is about, in a word or two, shown above it.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "multiple": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Whether the person may choose several.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "options": {
          "description": "The options to choose from; none, the person answers in words.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/Choice"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "question": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "question"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "Choice": {
      "description": "An option a question offers.",
      "properties": {
        "description": {
          "description": "What it means, where the label alone does not say.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "label": {
          "description": "What the person picks, in a few words.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "label"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  },
  "properties": {
    "questions": {
      "items": {
        "$ref": "#/$defs/Asked"
      },
      "maxItems": 8,
      "minItems": 1,
      "type": "array"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "questions"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool read
Reads a file, or lists a directory, a / after each directory's name. Text comes as it is, without line numbers, at most 2000 lines or 48 KB a page; a line longer than 2000 characters is cut. A note after the page gives the lines it holds and the offset to read on from. An image is shown to you; a PDF or other binary file is named, not shown.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "limit": {
      "description": "How many lines, or entries.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "offset": {
      "description": "The first line, or entry, from 1.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "description": "The file or directory, relative to the workspace, or absolute; ~ the home folder.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool shell
Runs a command line in bash in the workspace, its input closed. Gives its output and errors as one stream, then how it ended: [exit N], the signal that ended it, or its time run out. It may run 120 seconds, or up to 600 with `timeout_seconds`; one that does not end by itself — a server, a watcher — belongs in a terminal. In the default mode it runs in a sandbox that writes only the workspace and the temporary folders; one that must write beyond them — installing for the whole machine, writing elsewhere — is run again with `escalate` and a justification, and the person decides.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "command": {
      "description": "The command line.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "escalate": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Run it beyond the sandbox, once the person lets it.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "justification": {
      "description": "Why it must write beyond the sandbox, in a sentence the person reads.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "timeout_seconds": {
      "description": "How long it may run, in seconds; 120 unless given.",
      "format": "uint64",
      "maximum": 600,
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "command"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool terminal
Works the thread's terminals, which the person sees beside the chat. open runs a command line that does not end by itself — a server, a watcher — in a terminal of its own, in the workspace, held as the shell's commands are; it gives the terminal's number and its screen after a moment, and when the command ends of itself you are told how, with its last screen. read gives what a terminal shows, the person's too; write types to a terminal you opened (`\u0003` for Ctrl-C, `\r` for Enter) and gives its screen after; close ends one you opened. For a command that ends by itself, use shell.
--- parameters
{
  "oneOf": [
    {
      "description": "Runs a command line in a terminal of its own.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "open",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "command": {
          "description": "The command line.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "escalate": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Run it beyond the sandbox, once the person lets it.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "justification": {
          "description": "Why it must write beyond the sandbox, in a sentence the person reads.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "command"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "What a terminal of the thread shows, the person's too.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "read",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Types to a terminal the model opened: keys as text, `\\u0003` for Ctrl-C, `\\r` for Enter.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "write",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal",
        "text"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Closes a terminal the model opened, ending what it runs.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "close",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  ]
}

=== tool todo
Keeps the list of the steps of your work, which the person sees above the chat. Give the whole list each time: it replaces the one before. Keep one step in_progress, mark each completed as soon as it is done, and add the steps you find on the way. Use it for work of three steps or more, or where the person asks for several things; not for one simple step or a question.
--- parameters
{
  "$defs": {
    "Todo": {
      "description": "A step of the work.",
      "properties": {
        "content": {
          "description": "What the step is, in a few words.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "status": {
          "$ref": "#/$defs/TodoStatus"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "content",
        "status"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "TodoStatus": {
      "description": "Where a step stands.",
      "enum": [
        "pending",
        "in_progress",
        "completed"
      ],
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "properties": {
    "todos": {
      "description": "Every step, in order: the list that replaces the one before.",
      "items": {
        "$ref": "#/$defs/Todo"
      },
      "type": "array"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "todos"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool write
Writes a file whole, making it and its directories where they are missing. Gives how many lines it added and took out. To change part of a file, use edit.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "content": {
      "description": "What the file holds after.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "path": {
      "description": "The file, relative to the workspace, or absolute; ~ the home folder.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path",
    "content"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}
